course: dictionary
lesson: 432
title: Arbitrary: chosen without a good reason
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Arbitrary means chosen by chance, or because someone wants it, and not for a good reason or rule. «Most words are arbitrary: the link between a word and its meaning is a habit of the speakers, not something in nature.» The clue is «not something in nature»: nothing makes a word fit its meaning, so the choice is arbitrary.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Most words are chosen for no good reason» still makes sense, so you have the right idea. The word is an adjective, so it can describe a rule, a choice or a number.
explain: In maths, an arbitrary number is any number you pick, such as in «take an arbitrary point». Your teacher is not choosing one special point. Any point will do, so a rule that works for it must work for all points.
explain: When a rule or punishment is arbitrary, it is unfair because power is used without rules and there is no good reason behind it. Arbitrary rules often feel unjust.
fact: Arbitrary means chosen by chance or by someone's wish, not for a good reason or rule.
fact: In maths, an arbitrary number is any number you pick, to show that a rule works for every number.
fact: An arbitrary punishment is unfair, because unfair power is used without rules.
fact: «Arbitrary» is an adjective, and its comparative form is more arbitrary.
fact: A good check is to swap in «chosen for no good reason» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Most words are arbitrary: the link between a word and its meaning is a habit of the speakers, not something in nature.» What does arbitrary mean here? | Very important and carefully planned | Found in nature and always the same | *Chosen by habit, not for a good reason | Always the same in every language
exercise: In maths, what is an arbitrary number? | A number you find in a book | *Any number you pick, to show a rule works for all | A number that is always even | A number that is very large
exercise: Which of these is an arbitrary punishment? | *Given with no rule and no fair reason | A small fine written in the school rules | Given because of a rule everyone agreed | Given after a fair trial, with clear rules
test: Which word means chosen by chance or someone's wish, not for a good reason? | Ancient | Careful | *Arbitrary | Glum
test: Ben says the school rule «no shoes on Tuesdays» is arbitrary. What does he mean? | It is a rule written by the best teachers | It is a very old rule | It is a rule that everyone loves | *It is a rule with no good reason behind it
exam: Sam says arbitrary means a careful choice made after long thought. Is he right? | Yes, it means the same as ancient | Yes, it means careful and wise | Not sure, so skip it | *No, arbitrary means chosen by chance or wish, not after careful thought
